  "full_text": "Hello and welcome back to JD science. In today's video we want to look at percentage by mass. This builds on what we learnt about relative atomic mass and relative formula mass. We will explain what percentage by mass means, how it is calculated and work through an example. You will also have an opportunity to try a question before we go through the answer together. Let's begin. What is percentage by mass? Percentage by mass tells us what proportion of the total mass of a compound comes from a particular element. For example, water contains hydrogen and oxygen. Although water contains two hydrogen atoms and only one oxygen atom, oxygen contributes most of the mass becauselandish by mass allows us to calculate exactly how much of the total mass is contributed by each element. How do we calculate percentage by mass? To calculate the percentage by mass of an element in a compound, we use the following formula. Percentage by mass equals the total relative atomic mass contribution of the element divided by the relative formula mass of the compound multiplied by 100. In simpler terms, we first calculate the relative formula mass of the whole compound. Next, we calculate the mass contribution of the element we are interested in. We then divide that contribution by the total relative formula mass and multiply by 100. Let's apply this method to an example. Worked example 1. Water. Calculate the percentage by mass of oxygen in water. The chemical formula of water is H2O. The relative atomic masses are hydrogen 1 and oxygen 16. First, calculate the relative formula mass of water. 2 multiplied by 1 plus 16 gives 18. Therefore the relative formula mass of water is 18. Next, identify the mass contribution of oxygen. There is one oxygen atom with a relative atomic mass of 16. Therefore the contribution of oxygen is 16. Now divide 16 by 18 and multiply by 100. This gives approximately 88.9 percent. Therefore oxygen makes up approximately 88.9 percent of the mass of water. Worked example 2. Carbon dioxide. Let's consider another example. Calculate the percentage by mass of carbon in carbon dioxide. Carbon dioxide has the formula CO2. The relative atomic masses are carbon 12 and oxygen 16. First, calculate the relative formula mass. 12 plus 2 multiplied by 16 gives 44. Next, identify the mass contribution of carbon. There is one carbon atom so its contribution is 12. Divide 12 by 44 and multiply by 100. This gives approximately 27.3 percent. Therefore carbon makes up approximately 27.3 percent of the mass of carbon dioxide. Practice question. Now it's your turn. Calculate the percentage by mass of calcium in calcium carbonate, CaCO3. The relative atomic masses are calcium 40, carbon 12 and oxygen 16. Pause the video and try the question. Let's check your answer. First, calculate the relative formula mass of calcium carbonate. 40 plus 12 plus 3 multiplied by 16 gives 100. Next, identify the mass contribution of calcium. There is one calcium atom with a a relative atomic mass of 40. Therefore divide 40 by 100 and multiply by 100. This gives 40 percent. The and percentage by mass of calcium in calcium carbonate is 40 percent. Well done if you got that correct. Closing. That brings us to the end of today's lesson on percentage by mass. Remember the key is to calculate the relative formula mass correctly and identify the total mass contribution of the element you are interested in."
